To configure your Jenkins to connect to a GitHub webhook, follow these steps to enable automatic builds when changes are pushed to a GitHub repository:

✅ Step 1: Install Required Jenkins Plugins
Ensure the following plugins are installed:

Git plugin

GitHub plugin

GitHub Integration Plugin (optional but useful)

GitHub Branch Source Plugin

Go to:
Jenkins Dashboard → Manage Jenkins → Plugins → Available
Search and install if not already present.

✅ Step 2: Create Jenkins Job
Go to Jenkins Dashboard → New Item

Enter a name and choose:

Freestyle project or

Pipeline project (depending on your setup)

Click OK

✅ Step 3: Configure Source Code Management
In your Jenkins job:

Go to Configure

Under Source Code Management, choose Git

Paste your GitHub repository URL

Provide credentials if it's private

Example:
https://github.com/your-username/your-repo.git

✅ Step 4: Enable GitHub Webhook Trigger
Scroll to Build Triggers

Select: ✅ GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling

✅ Step 5: Configure Webhook in GitHub
Go to your GitHub repository

Click on Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook

Set:

Payload URL:
http://<your-jenkins-domain>/github-webhook/

Example (local Jenkins via ngrok):
http://a1b2c3d4.ngrok.io/github-webhook/

Content type: application/json

Which events?: Choose Just the push event or more if needed

Secret (optional): Can be added for security (make sure Jenkins and GitHub use the same)

Click Add Webhook

✅ Step 6: Test the Webhook
Make a commit/push to your GitHub repo

Go to GitHub → Webhooks → Recent Deliveries to see if the webhook fired

Jenkins should automatically trigger the job if everything is set correctly

